e solution  progressives like myself have been  pushed to the margins there's been it's  been a  sense of saying you've you know you've  got to accept the market there was a  propagation of what i call fatalism in  the book and oddly enough trump broke  fatalism he made voting count he  empowered his followers  and at the beginning of the biden  presidency there was a sense of uh an  intelligent empowerment taking place as  a counterweight and i thought that that  is historic what you're doing in america  the potential i know everybody in  america at the moment all progressing  them are feeling very down there's the  supreme court decisions are making it  even worse there's a sense of pessimism  in the air  but the thing is biden won with a seven  million plurality  trump got the largest vote any sitting  president ever got in history got four  and a half million more votes than obama  did and obama got  69.5 million the largest ever trump got  74 million and then got 81 million  so we if i can put it like that we  progressives are the majority we've got  to get our act together the system is  rigged against us  and and so i felt we had to address this  and the question i asked myself  is what given the defeat of the left  given the defeat of progressives  given the defeating effect of the  socialist argument of the trade union  movement  why are progressives so strong how come  there was a seven million  majority  what is the strength i can understand  the weaknesses and then i look to this  question of what i call the humanization  it's feminism black lives matter human  rights underneath marketization all  these forces against the market were  growing and i think that  from the point of view whether it's  putin or whether it's trump uh that's  the or she in china or modi in india  that's their threat they're there they  understand the threat of real democracy  uh in a way that the  i think that the leadership of the  progressive movement certainly leads to  biden leadership doesn't fully  understand also is rather frightened of  and we've got to get our act together  and that's what i've been trying to  explore and that's why i'm very pleased  to be on your program i want to talk i  think there's a bigger conversation a  worldwide conversation is needed to sort  out how we go from here well it's  interesting when you talk about the um  olive branches extended by joe biden to  progressives you know what i think my  audience which is mostly progressive  um  did not feel as though there were really  too many olive branches i mean i'll give  i'll give you my perspective at the time  which was yes i voted for joe biden i  believed that on paper joe biden ran on  the most progressive platform of any  democratic nominee i mean if you look at  obama in 2012 there and hillary in 2016.  on paper for what joe biden was  promising it was to the left of that to  some degree but also there was this  feeling that he his heart really wasn't  in a lot of the things he was sort of  agreeing to like for example joe biden  talked about maybe some student loan  forgiveness i didn't believe for a  second that that was really something  biden was interested in and he's really  not done anything on it cannabis  decriminalization or legalization he  sort of paid a little bit of lip service  to it  i didn't think he really cared about  that and he's not really taking any  action on it other than i think about a  hundred people uh whose records have  been expunged for for cannabis related  crimes or something like that so i went  in there with eyes wide open  knowing that this was the better option  i voted for him i think he's done some  good things but at the same time i i  don't know that the majority of my  progressive audience feels as though the  olive branches were serious or that  they've been followed through on  yeah well i think this is this is right  but i think that so there's different  things here there's biden himself i  think the vine himself  was actually shocked by  trump's uh willingness to reach out to  fascists and i don't think biden is a  racist i think that's really important  and i think that his he genuinely  was pleased to put a black woman in the  supreme court i think that he does  believe in in integration he is he's a  multi-racial figure i mean that's a very  deep and a very important issue and i  think that he reached out with his heart  wasn't in it but the point was and what  he stated and he understood is that  without the support of the  transformative promise  of the of the bernie sanders movement he  wouldn't have won  so is that i guess progress even if it  was cynical it's still coming from my  point of view of from our point of view  we've got to say okay okay right we've  got something going for us here right of  course we're not looking biden is a  creature of the american political  establishment yeah  of the last 40 years he pre-dates  neoliberalism he predates the  marketization but um and and we've  there's got to be a big generational  change taking place so so okay we've got  some strength here and how do we build  on this strength and i think one of our  problems has been one of the problems in  america but it's also a problem here in  the uk is too much looking at the  president at the center you know the the  right built its strength on the in the  states it built its strength locally  so it's building locally as well  we've got to be able to del there are  there's a very big progressive caucus in  the in the congress and i made a short  film called u.s progressives on the  knife edge i went to washington to  investigate the argument of my book to  test it if you like i talked to  a representative jamie raskin i talked  to pramila jayapal who's the chair of  the congressional  um  progressive caucus and i talked to  representative ro kahana who represents  silicon valley from california and they  were very interesting and articulate and  reaching out it seemed to me these are  not kind of you know um  they're seeking to gain popular support  but there isn't a progressive gathering  of governors of the us states  there's a weakness our weakness isn't  just oh well look biden isn't delivering  for us it's we've got to think okay this  is really serious we're up against  fighting we're not going to stop this  guy  and and those forces we've got to build  the alternative in a way that's credible  and build on that majority  in terms of building and alliances you  you talk about  the need for the progressive left to  ally with centrists to achieve  substantive  progress in in some senses and i think  the question of alliances is an  interesting one it's also very different  depending on whether a country has a  parliamentary system with proportional  representation or a system like the  united states or whatever the case may  be when it comes to  or i guess even thinking about it more  broadly  if the centrists  don't really want the things the  progressives want who's compromising in  those alliances  yeah well this is a very good question  one thing which i think is shifting is  that  they are beginning to understand that  without being in alliance with  progressives the center is going to lose  that's a very big shift  up until now whether it was under blair  or clinton or bill clinton or even obama  they felt they didn't need they felt  they didn't need the left they all they  needed was corporate power and corporate  support and corporate competence and  that would be enough and they could  they could rely upon the quiescence  of the population trump has made voting  count he's empowered his supporters and  unless  the the forces the democratic forces  that are opposed to trump unless we  empower our supporters we're finished  and that empowerment if you take the the  supreme court decision right on row  now what does a woman right to choose  mean in my view a woman's right to  choose is  their right to life  i think life is about choice i think one  of the extraordinary things about that  larger feminist movement is that it  empowers all of us i think men included  in in in in greater control over our  lives and one of the great historic  problems of the center and the liberals  that you talk to is they just say we'll  give you a bit of welfare we'll give you  some me maybe we'll give you students  though maybe we won't but whatever it is  you've got to be passive  they rely upon our passivity and i think  that trump has shown that that game is  over so we need to build on that and i i  think that we've got to be thinking  afresh but my argument it's not that i'm  an optimist  it's that we haven't lost yet and we  need to think about what the forces the  positive forces that there are and i  think that this sense of saying okay  we're going to show you what democratic  empowerment is really about  we uh in in the few minutes we have left  i want to talk a little bit about the  next two to four years you know in uh  many in the media always like to say  this is the most interesting important  and significant election of our time  because it benefits the media because  that sounds interesting but in i've used  this example before in 2012 in the  united states when mitt romney was  challenging barack obama's re-election i  i voted for barack obama and i thought  he was the better choice but it didn't  feel like it was the most consequential  election of our time it was sort of like  yes taxes on the rich will go down a  little bit if mitt romney wins and he's  socially conservative and i'm not but it  didn't feel as though there was as much  riding on it we now have a situation  where boris johnson resigning in the uk  the rise of the right-wing nationalist  parties in europe we have now in 2022  here in the united states the first  trump-era election where trump is not  president nor on the ballot and there's  this real question as to whether his  constituents will remain engaged 